    2. Define a wedge/diamond shape via per-row left/right boundaries (each boundary changes by 0 or 1 per row, overall height 5-7, max width 3-5). Position it touching exactly 2 grid edges.
    3. The outline = border pixels of this filled shape. Compute outer contour = bg pixels 4-adjacent to outline. This is the output.
    4. Assign colors - pick outline color, optionally one fill color, and a marker color, all with distinct pixel counts. Fill shape concentrically (outline on border, fill inside, marker innermost).
    5. Verify outline is single 8-connected component, marker count < any other fg count, and outer contour is non-empty. This is the input.
spec:
  task_type: move_to_match (translate sprites to align marker pixel with matching border anchor)
  grid:
    size: 10-22 rows x 8-22 cols (aspect roughly rectangular, one side 10-14, other 18-22 [observed])
    bg: most common color (any color 1-8)
  anchor (dst):
    count: 1-3 distinct colors, each a single isolated border pixel
    placement: lies on a border row/col (top/bottom/left/right); not a corner [observed]
    isolation: all 4-neighbors equal bg (no other non-bg touching within Manhattan-1 or diagonal)
    axis_rule: top/bottom edge => sprite slides horizontally (match column); left/right edge => slides vertically (match row)
    duplicate_anchors: a color may appear at 2 opposite-edge anchors (same row/col) [observed, test only]
  sprite (src):
    count: 3-5 multi-color connected components in the interior
    colors_per_sprite: 2 (one body color + one marker color); rarely 3 [observed, 1 case]
    marker: exactly 1 pixel, color = least-frequent color inside sprite, must match one anchor color
    body: 4-13 cells of a single non-bg, non-marker color; irregular shape (hollow rect, plus, L, ring, etc.); bounding box 2x3 to 5x7
    marker_position: adjacent (4-neighbor) to body cells, typically on body perimeter or inside hollow; never isolated from body
    connectivity: 8-connected component; interior of sprite may contain bg cells (hollow)
    placement: fully inside grid; sprites do not touch each other or anchors (gap >=1 bg on all sides)
    symmetry: body often has LR or UD symmetry [observed, ~60%]
  extras:
    distractor_singletons: occasionally isolated interior pixels sharing an anchor color act as extra "mini-sprites" that also slide [observed, test_0 only]
    marker_anchor_match: every sprite's marker color must equal some anchor color; multiple sprites may share the same marker color
  transform:
    per_sprite: translate by (dr, dc) so marker aligns with anchor along the axis dictated by anchor edge
    clipping: out-of-bounds pixels after slide are dropped; bg cells under destination are overwritten; non-bg collisions are NOT overwritten
    originals_cleared: source cells become bg; anchors stay in place
  example_layout: |
    ... ... (scatter sprites in interior; each marker color matches one border anchor)
    .888.....
    .848.....    <- sprite marker=4 matches bottom anchor at col 5
    .........
    ....4....    <- bottom anchor (non-corner border)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first (build settled/aligned state, then invert by scattering sprites away from anchors)
  steps:
    - Choose grid H in [10,22], W in [8,22]; pick bg in [1..8].
    - Choose 1-3 anchor colors (distinct from bg). For each: place a single isolated non-corner border pixel (top/bottom => horizontal-axis anchor; left/right => vertical-axis anchor). Ensure 8-neighborhood (within grid) is all bg.
    - Decide number of sprites N in [3,5]; assign each a marker color from the anchor color set (multiple sprites may share a color).
    - For each sprite, build body in the OUTPUT position (aligned with anchor):
        * Pick a body color (distinct from bg and marker and other sprites' bodies when possible).
        * Generate an irregular small shape (size 4-13, bbox up to 5x7); shape types: hollow rect/ring, plus, L, T, frame-with-gap. Prefer LR or UD symmetry ~60% of the time.
        * Place marker as 1 pixel 4-adjacent to body (perimeter or inside hollow).
        * Position sprite so marker cell equals (anchor_row, chosen_col) for top/bottom anchors, or (chosen_row, anchor_col) for left/right anchors. The "free" axis coordinate is chosen so the sprite fits (with optional partial clipping OK but keep body mostly inside).
        * Ensure sprite body and marker do not touch (8-neighbor) any anchor, any other sprite, or the border (leave >=1 bg gap except where slide clips allowed).
    - Optional (10-20% of grids): add 1-4 distractor singleton pixels of one anchor color scattered in interior (each isolated 8-neighbor), same color as a left/right anchor (they also slide vertically).
    - Invert: for each sprite (and distractor), pick a scatter displacement along the OPPOSITE axis of its slide (horizontal anchors => move sprite vertically away; vertical anchors => move horizontally away) by some random nonzero offset, keeping sprite inside grid and non-overlapping. Remove original aligned sprite pixels (set to bg), paste at scattered location. This yields the input.
    - Self-check: verify by running solver that solver(input) == output.
spec:
  grid: 8-30 rows x 8-30 cols
  bg: most common color (0 in train, 5 in test)
  seeds:
    count: 1-2 unique-color single pixels on the grid border
    position: typically at or near a corner [observed]
    each seed has a distinct color appearing exactly once in the grid
  lines:
    count: 1 per seed, each with a distinct line_color
    structure: 8-connected meandering path from seed through the grid (1px wide, uses diagonal steps so NOT 4-connected)
    length: 6-45 line_color pixels per seed's component
    the line is part of a larger 8-connected non-bg component that includes frame loops
  frame_loops:
    count: 1-9 total across the grid (single-color-boundary cavities)
    shape: closed ring of a single frame_color enclosing bg pixels; cavity is mostly 2x2 rectangular (27/36 observed), occasionally larger
    each loop's boundary is entirely one frame_color; the line_color pixels touch the loop's OUTER edges (8-adjacent) without bordering the cavity directly
    1-4 distinct frame colors per example; a frame_color can form multiple loops
  connectivity:
    loops attached to the seed's line (8-connected via line_color) get their cavity filled with seed_color
    filled: 1-8 per example; unfilled (distractor): 0-5
    at least 1 unfilled cavity exists in 5/6 examples [observed]
  distractors:
    0-1 disconnected 8-connected non-bg components with no seed; may contain line+frame structure or be a single frame loop
  output: same grid with each connected cavity flood-filled by its seed's color

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size, bg color, 1-2 seeds with unique colors, and per-seed line_color
    2. For each seed, draw a meandering 8-connected line path from the seed's border position inward, using diagonal and orthogonal steps
    3. Along each line, attach 2-5 frame loops (closed rings of a frame_color enclosing a 2x2 bg cavity); the line touches the loop at 1-2 outer edge positions (8-adjacent to the frame ring)
    4. Place 0-3 additional distractor loops (same frame_color or different) NOT 8-connected to any line
    5. Optionally add 0-1 disconnected non-bg component as a distractor network
    6. Verify: each cavity has single-color boundary; cavities connected to a line are fillable; at least 1 unfilled cavity exists
    7. Output is derived by flood-filling each connected cavity with its seed_color
spec:
  grid: 15x15 [observed]
  background: BLACK
  colors: frame=RED(2), marker=GREY(5), output_fill=YELLOW(4) [observed]
  frames:
    count: 2, in diagonally opposite quadrants (top-left/bot-right or top-right/bot-left)
    structure: |
      3x3 bbox; center=marker, 7 of 8 surrounding cells=frame_color,
      1 gap at cardinal position (N/S/E/W of center) = bg
    gap: the cell adjacent to marker through the gap is the doorway; one more step in same direction is the exterior cell
    exterior_cell: must be in-bounds and bg; marker needs >=2 cells clearance to grid edge in gap direction
    separation: >=5 rows and >=5 cols between marker centers
  corridor:
    endpoints: the two exterior cells (always differ in both row and col)
    monotone_moves: only steps toward the other endpoint (at most down/up + left/right)
    fill: union of all bg cells reachable from both endpoints via monotone steps within the bounding box, treating frame/marker pixels as blockers
  output:
    paint corridor fill cells + both doorway gap cells YELLOW; keep frame and marker unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid 15x15. Place two marker positions in diagonally opposite quadrants with >=5 row and >=5 col separation between them, and each >=2 cells from the nearest edge in at least one cardinal direction.
    2. For each marker, choose a gap direction (N/S/E/W) such that the exterior cell (marker + 2*gap_dir) is in-bounds. Build the 3x3 frame (8 surrounding cells = frame_color, except the gap cell = bg).
    3. Compute exterior cells. Verify they differ in both row and col (diagonal corridor). If not, re-pick gap directions.
    4. Run solver's monotone BFS from both endpoints; compute the yellow region. Verify the path is non-empty (it always will be if no extra obstacles exist and endpoints differ in both dimensions).
    5. Return the input grid (frames + markers on black bg); output is computed by solver.

